        "header": "3Decoding Without a Decoder",
        "images": [
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22287/2611.22287v2/dwd_arch.png",
                "caption": "Figure 2:Standard latent generation vs. DWD.(a)A standard generator samples a denoised latent and maps it to pixels with a decoder.(b)DWD retains the denoising head for latent denoising and adds a parallel DWD head that produces the final pixels only at the final step, bypassing this decoder. DWD finetunes the pretrained generator’s final blocks and heads (fire icon), while freezing all previous blocks (ice icon). Timettand conditioning𝒄{\\bm{c}}are omitted for clarity.",
                "position": 297
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22287/2611.22287v2/probing.png",
                "caption": "Figure 3:Last-layer tokens support more accurate linear pixel reconstruction.We fit patch-wise linear probes from denoised latents𝒛^0\\hat{\\bm{z}}_{0}and last-layer tokens𝒉{\\bm{h}}to ground-truth pixels, keeping the pretrained models frozen.(a)Held-out relative RMSE, with percentages showing error reduction over the latent probe.(b, c)Probes of last-layer tokens preserve finer image detail and reduce video ghosting before finetuning.",
                "position": 340
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "header": "4Experiments",
        "images": [
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22287/2611.22287v2/dwd_final_t_compare.png",
                "caption": "Figure 4:A nonzero final timestep is necessary for the DWD quality gain.(a)Attfinal=0t_{\\mathrm{final}}{=}0, where the DWD head receives a fully denoised input, FID matches or trails the baseline’s, so the gain vanishes; a small positive timestep recovers it. Larger circles mark the defaults used in Table1.(b)Qualitative results also confirm the trend: DWD at its default timestep recovers skin and fabric texture, while DWD attfinal=0t_{\\mathrm{final}}{=}0is as smooth as the baseline. The large panels are zoomed crops, with the full image inset.",
                "position": 605
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22287/2611.22287v2/dwd_t2i_qual.png",
                "caption": "Figure 5:Text-to-image comparisons.Each pair shows the baseline (left) and its DWD extension (right) for the same prompt and seed.(a)On the latent FLUX.2 klein models, DWD closely matches the baseline’s content and texture without the decoder (zoom in to view detail).(b)On the pixel AnisoFlow model, DWD preserves the composition while recovering photorealistic texture in bark, foliage, and clouds. The large panels are zoomed crops, with the full image inset.",
                "position": 760
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22287/2611.22287v2/dwd_t2v_qual.png",
                "caption": "Figure 6:Text-to-video comparisons (BF16).Three frames from the baseline Wan2.1 14B (left) and its DWD extension (right) for the same prompt and seed, with two zoomed crops per strip. The baseline decoder warps the wire mesh into wavy, uneven lines (top) and breaks the dense pipes into speckle (bottom); neither defect is static, both shifting from frame to frame. DWD keeps the mesh regular and the pipe structure intact, and holds them steady as the camera tracks across the scene.",
                "position": 952
            }
        ]
    },
